vel carbon taxes or  cap and trade schemes i was wondering  what role these could play in supporting  or funding some of these policies  assuming that federal action you know  federal carbon tax or a federal traffic  cap and trade scheme is probably pretty  unlikely at this stage  well i can talk a little bit about the  um you know the clean power plan um and  uh you know sort of the the state the  federal media that states uh meet  certain levels of emissions reductions  um you know again while again widespread  cap and trade policy is not very likely  um you know this is this is sort of a  one of those one of the ways in which  the federal government is setting  standards um we are in the process we  think that establishing financing  programs  could be an effective uh compliance  mechanism if in fact um  we can design them in such a way to  encourage uptake um  but uh the challenge uh  from for  helping them to meet that standard is um  finding the most appropriate way to  measure and evaluate the effectiveness  of these programs but we are in the  process of developing technical  assistance  around  how that could be accomplished  so the other thing that i'll add on to  that is that i believe that california's  new policy  that they're putting in place is going  to actually provide a source of capital  that they're going to be able to put  back into programs they have  environmental benefits i'm not sure how  much that is going to go to  energy programs or exactly what type  people are also looking at it for  environmental cleanup and some other  issues as well but if you have a policy  that is going to be raising money from  people who are from businesses that are  in compliance then that's the potential  source of fund capitalization  great okay the next question um also  comes from a different daniel  and he's asking about any experiences or  any knowledge out there amongst the  panelists for our financing models  really looking at low-income residents  aroun